WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:03.780
I think that's a big thing that I
preach, which is you don't need to be

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:06.560
the best.  You just have to have fun
with it; it's a hobby for everyone.

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:10.320
Some people get so strung out and
so annoyed and worried at becoming

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:12.320
a perfectionist, and especially
when they get in front of a

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:13.900
camera, so I try to not do that.

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:18.399
Today I have a mystery guest, who
goes by the name of Language Simp.

00:00:18.770 --> 00:00:19.849
I don't know what his name is.

00:00:20.069 --> 00:00:21.469
I presume he has a name.

00:00:21.479 --> 00:00:25.859
Maybe he was born Language
Simp. But he is a motivator, an

00:00:25.859 --> 00:00:27.999
entertainer, a tremendous polyglot.

00:00:28.059 --> 00:00:33.379
He's a former user of LingQ and he
claims to like my videos and like LingQ.

00:00:33.379 --> 00:00:35.569
So for all of these reasons,
I want to talk to him.

00:00:35.610 --> 00:00:39.539
So first of all, Language Simp,
can you tell us what your name is?

00:00:39.929 --> 00:00:40.750
Or is that a secret?

00:00:41.449 --> 00:00:43.980
You're trying to get my
personal information out of me

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:44.970
right from the start, Steve?

00:00:44.970 --> 00:00:45.989
I wasn't expecting that!

00:00:46.139 --> 00:00:47.019
Give me an alias.

00:00:47.050 --> 00:00:47.839
Give me an alias.

00:00:47.889 --> 00:00:49.870
I say that my legal birth
name is Language Simp.

00:00:49.879 --> 00:00:52.769
So for the case of this interview,
we will confirm that it is,

00:00:52.769 --> 00:00:54.050
in fact, my legal birth name.

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:55.379
But I am curious, Steve...

00:00:55.379 --> 00:00:57.959
Do you know what the word "simp" means?

00:00:57.989 --> 00:01:00.530
Well, I'm assuming it has
something to do with "simple".

00:01:00.610 --> 00:01:01.470
"Simpleton" maybe?

00:01:01.849 --> 00:01:02.679
It does not.

00:01:02.789 --> 00:01:04.039
It's very Gen Z.

00:01:04.039 --> 00:01:07.730
And I'll say that kind of the whole crux
of my channel is Gen Z humor and getting

00:01:07.730 --> 00:01:09.369
Gen Z type people into languages.

00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:11.220
That would be Gen Zed in Canada, okay?

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:11.829
Gen Zed.

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:13.010
Just so you know.

00:01:13.149 --> 00:01:15.960
I don't even call English "English."

00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:18.240
I call English "American,"
so I don't know about that.

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:22.289
But, basically, a "simp" is
someone who goes after women, but

00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:23.940
tthey're not very good at it.

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:26.349
They're kind of desperate; that type thing.

00:01:26.350 --> 00:01:28.549
So in my sense, I'm
desperate for languages.

00:01:28.580 --> 00:01:29.090
A simp...

00:01:29.355 --> 00:01:32.555
You can be a simp for
someone, but I'm a simp for languages.

00:01:32.735 --> 00:01:33.305
That's good.

00:01:33.345 --> 00:01:36.385
At least in that case,
it's one sided.

00:01:36.395 --> 00:01:39.794
You can pursue the language and you
don't need the language to respond.

00:01:39.824 --> 00:01:42.734
Whereas if you go for a woman
and she doesn't want you,

00:01:42.744 --> 00:01:43.655
you're kind of out of luck.

00:01:43.715 --> 00:01:47.505
But in the case of languages, even
if I'm not speaking the language well

00:01:47.505 --> 00:01:50.565
and I'm struggling, I can just have
fun with it at, at any level, you know,

00:01:50.565 --> 00:01:52.115
I don't need to be perfectly fluent.

00:01:52.115 --> 00:01:54.495
And I think that's a big
thing that I preach, which is:

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:56.830
you don't need to be the best.

00:01:56.830 --> 00:01:57.980
You just have to have fun with it.

00:01:57.980 --> 00:01:59.060
It's a hobby for everyone.

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:02.860
Some people get so strung out and
so annoyed and worried at becoming a

00:02:02.860 --> 00:02:05.969
perfectionist, and especially when they get
in front of a camera. So I try not to do that.

00:02:06.109 --> 00:02:07.329
I couldn't agree more.

00:02:07.330 --> 00:02:08.829
I think 100% that's true.

00:02:09.099 --> 00:02:12.260
And the other fun thing about
being a Language Simp is that

00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:16.240
you can pursue five languages and
none of the languages get jealous.

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:19.520
Whereas if you are pursuing five
women, you might start getting

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:20.730
into trouble at some point.

00:02:20.850 --> 00:02:21.210
Yes.

00:02:21.270 --> 00:02:24.190
I've never pursued five women, but
five languages at the same time?

00:02:24.190 --> 00:02:24.480
Yes.

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:24.810
Although

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:27.470
it gets a little rough when you're
studying three at the same time,

00:02:27.470 --> 00:02:31.890
which I knew you were doing Turkish,
Farsi, and Arabic at the same time.

00:02:31.890 --> 00:02:32.750
That must have, uh... I gave up!

00:02:34.509 --> 00:02:37.920
Well, it's good to admit that you can give
up and not be embarrassed to share that.

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:42.049
I've put Turkish
on the back burner, and I want to

00:02:42.050 --> 00:02:45.280
get enough exposure to the Arabic
script that I get good at it.

00:02:45.330 --> 00:02:48.640
With Arabic, too, you've got
Egyptian, Levantine, Fusha, so

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:50.080
there's enough there to keep me busy.

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:52.290
Actually, I was looking at
Turkish just yesterday, so...

00:02:52.675 --> 00:02:56.145
Any of the languages, I'm sure you've
had the same experience, that you start a

00:02:56.145 --> 00:02:58.185
language, you leave it, it's always there.

00:02:58.235 --> 00:03:01.575
It's like a girlfriend that you
can go and visit whenever you want.

00:03:01.585 --> 00:03:04.715
It's not a love affair, but you
go there and you enjoy being

00:03:04.715 --> 00:03:06.235
together and it's fine.

00:03:06.285 --> 00:03:08.924
Well, as long as you didn't move
on to another woman, but yeah,

00:03:08.925 --> 00:03:09.915
I understand what you're saying.

00:03:10.484 --> 00:03:12.304
Depends what you mean by "move on to" too.

00:03:13.904 --> 00:03:14.684
Yeah, for sure.

00:03:14.934 --> 00:03:16.635
A funny thing with the Arabic script...

00:03:16.645 --> 00:03:18.725
I'm someone who loves alphabets.

00:03:18.755 --> 00:03:20.025
I don't know if you've seen my videos.

00:03:20.025 --> 00:03:20.685
I actually

00:03:20.860 --> 00:03:24.240
recited the Arabic and Russian
alphabets for 10 hours straight,

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:26.350
just continually on livestream.

00:03:26.350 --> 00:03:29.500
And a lot of people enjoyed that,
but I actually can't read

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:32.210
very well, and that's why I stopped
using LingQ back in the day.

00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:35.549
I have a lazy eye. I don't know if you
can see that very well,

00:03:35.629 --> 00:03:37.759
but I've got an eye kind
of going off to the side.

00:03:37.850 --> 00:03:40.560
And it makes it really difficult
to read. And it's gotten a lot worse.

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:43.040
But back in the day, I used to...

00:03:43.070 --> 00:03:46.530
I was studying Russian on LingQ
for about a year

00:03:46.580 --> 00:03:48.099
I was in a lot of pain because it's

00:03:48.110 --> 00:03:48.929
hard to read.

00:03:48.930 --> 00:03:49.980
It stresses my eyes.

00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:54.680
And instead of giving up, like a normal
person, or just going to audio only

00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:59.430
content, I doubled down and started
to do it for three hours a day, timed.

00:03:59.669 --> 00:04:02.880
So there was a point in my life where
I wore an eye patch on my right eye.

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:05.989
And I was just using LingQ and
just studying the Mini Stories

00:04:05.989 --> 00:04:09.489
and stuff for three hours a day.
And torturing myself. But it worked!

00:04:09.570 --> 00:04:10.750
And my Russian is pretty decent.

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:14.950
You are a simp with an eyepatch.
I was a pirate. A simp pirate with

00:04:14.950 --> 00:04:18.849
an eyepatch using Steve Kaufman's
beautiful site. So... But no, I think

00:04:18.859 --> 00:04:23.060
three at once is a lot. Two at once,
I mean, it's kind of got advantages

00:04:23.060 --> 00:04:27.169
and disadvantages. The advantages of
focusing on one is you improve more

00:04:27.169 --> 00:04:28.549
quickly in the one where you're focused.

00:04:28.590 --> 00:04:32.359
The advantage of doing two is that you
get a bit of a break from one and you

00:04:32.359 --> 00:04:34.340
go to another, so things stay fresh.

00:04:34.460 --> 00:04:36.689
At the end of the day, I don't know
whether you're further ahead doing

00:04:36.689 --> 00:04:39.899
one at a time and then doing the
other, focusing on it, or whether

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:41.289
you're better off to do two at a time.

00:04:41.499 --> 00:04:41.779
I don't know.

00:04:41.959 --> 00:04:42.779
Personal preference.

00:04:42.859 --> 00:04:45.070
Yeah, I mean, you can definitely
study a few at a time, but

00:04:45.070 --> 00:04:46.210
it just slows the progress.

00:04:46.210 --> 00:04:48.429
But I always tell people,
sometimes people come to me, they

00:04:48.429 --> 00:04:51.090
say they feel this burnout and
they really don't want to continue.

00:04:51.299 --> 00:04:52.895
And I always say, "Just quit."

00:04:52.915 --> 00:04:53.485
"Take a break."

00:04:53.495 --> 00:04:56.585
As you said, when you come back to it,
you're not going to lose everything.

00:04:56.585 --> 00:04:59.415
I mean, maybe the few things
that you learned right at the end

00:04:59.415 --> 00:05:00.644
will be a little fuzzy, but...

00:05:01.195 --> 00:05:01.695
Take a break.

00:05:01.705 --> 00:05:02.504
Go study another.

00:05:02.504 --> 00:05:04.135
This is a hobby for everyone.

00:05:04.135 --> 00:05:07.625
I think a big part of my channel,
too, is kind of showing how...

00:05:07.805 --> 00:05:11.605
You get a lot of polyglots out there
who love to shock people or show off

00:05:11.605 --> 00:05:13.615
their skills in highly edited videos.

00:05:13.815 --> 00:05:16.915
And I like to show them, no,
it's fine if you make a mistake.

00:05:16.915 --> 00:05:17.795
It's fine if

00:05:18.224 --> 00:05:21.484
you're not perfect in a language.
You don't have to be that guy who

00:05:21.484 --> 00:05:25.245
studies French for three straight
years to become perfect at it.

00:05:25.495 --> 00:05:28.455
Do what you want, have fun
with it and see where it goes.

00:05:28.524 --> 00:05:31.595
First of all, for those who don't
know Language Simp, I'm going to

00:05:31.595 --> 00:05:33.574
leave a link in the description box.

00:05:33.614 --> 00:05:35.384
I've only heard you in Arabic.

00:05:35.385 --> 00:05:38.814
I've heard you in Russian. And you
are very good, very competent.

00:05:38.914 --> 00:05:42.895
Also, I'm surprised here in our
conversation to find out that

00:05:42.895 --> 00:05:45.894
you're actually quite a serious
guy. Because the persona

00:05:46.370 --> 00:05:51.659
that you find on your videos is very
much more sort of a goofy guy. A goofy

00:05:51.659 --> 00:05:57.569
guy who nevertheless motivates people to
learn languages by being goofy, which I

00:05:57.569 --> 00:06:01.109
think is good. Because at school sometimes
languages are presented as something

00:06:01.109 --> 00:06:03.209
very serious and we can't make mistakes.

00:06:03.450 --> 00:06:07.179
And if we get, you know, four out
of ten wrong, then that's bad.

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:08.670
And all of that stuff.

00:06:08.670 --> 00:06:11.450
And you basically ignore all
of that stuff and just enjoy your

00:06:11.450 --> 00:06:12.850
languages and do very well at it.

00:06:12.930 --> 00:06:13.950
Yeah, yeah, for sure.

00:06:14.070 --> 00:06:16.300
So how did you get started
in learning languages?

00:06:16.410 --> 00:06:21.179
I actually watched a World War II
movie back in the day, and I heard

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:22.980
French in it, and I just fell in love.

00:06:22.980 --> 00:06:25.180
I thought it was the most
sophisticated thing on the planet.

00:06:25.490 --> 00:06:27.990
Thought it was pretentious,
but in a good way.

00:06:27.990 --> 00:06:32.010
I kind of fantasized about being in
Paris in a nice cafe speaking this

00:06:32.340 --> 00:06:37.320
luxurious language to someone and feeling
all preppy. And so I started studying it

00:06:37.320 --> 00:06:42.360
on January 1, 2019. So that was the first
time I started studying a language. And

00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:44.540
I dedicated my life to it right away.

00:06:44.540 --> 00:06:48.820
And then I found you and some other
people online, and I just realized

00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:50.799
that people can learn languages.

00:06:50.870 --> 00:06:54.760
I didn't even know, as an American growing
up, everyone says, "Oh, you should learn

00:06:54.890 --> 00:06:57.569
Mexican!" But no one really did it.

00:06:57.570 --> 00:06:59.580
I never saw anyone who
was successful in that.

00:06:59.955 --> 00:07:03.835
So I decided, or I realized,
"Wow, this is actually possible!"

00:07:03.835 --> 00:07:06.615
People have done it, and it's
possible with multiple languages,

00:07:06.865 --> 00:07:08.005
and it can be a lifestyle.

00:07:08.025 --> 00:07:10.785
So, I fell in love with it, started
learning French, and never looked back.

00:07:10.785 --> 00:07:12.575
So which languages do you speak now?

00:07:12.635 --> 00:07:16.425
And you don't have to tell me
whether you're "B2" or "C1" or whatever.

00:07:16.425 --> 00:07:18.005
Just, which languages do you speak?

00:07:18.065 --> 00:07:21.905
I like to say that I speak languages
at a "D1" level, just to completely

00:07:21.915 --> 00:07:25.515
surpass that scale and just show people
that it's kind of nonsense in a way.

00:07:26.419 --> 00:07:32.469
So I'd say I'm D1 in French, Spanish,
Russian, Arabic, and then

00:07:32.469 --> 00:07:36.349
I've studied a bunch of others, flirted
with Portuguese and Danish and stuff.

00:07:36.349 --> 00:07:40.389
But I also... a big part of me is that I
don't like to claim that I "speak" languages

00:07:40.390 --> 00:07:41.870
because people get so worried about that.

00:07:41.919 --> 00:07:45.020
On the definition, the definition
of fluency, "Can I speak this?"

00:07:45.020 --> 00:07:47.640
And I like to surpass that
and say, have fun with it.

00:07:47.650 --> 00:07:48.950
So that's my answer.

00:07:48.969 --> 00:07:52.179
You know that Danish has the
largest number of phonemes of

00:07:52.179 --> 00:07:53.640
different sounds of any language?

00:07:53.920 --> 00:07:54.480
Oh, yeah.

00:07:54.480 --> 00:07:55.100
Oh, for sure.

00:07:55.100 --> 00:07:58.980
When I was an engineer... So I
actually quit my engineering job to be a

00:07:59.300 --> 00:08:02.349
YouTuber now, but when I was an engineer,
I worked with a bunch of Danes, and

00:08:02.350 --> 00:08:06.700
that's why I started studying Danish. And
yeah, they speak like, I don't even know.

00:08:07.040 --> 00:08:09.120
It's the most insane
thing I've ever heard.

00:08:09.415 --> 00:08:12.835
But, uh, when you said "phonemes," I
actually have no idea what that means

00:08:13.025 --> 00:08:15.305
because I also stay away from linguistics.

00:08:15.445 --> 00:08:15.845
Not a fan.

00:08:15.845 --> 00:08:19.184
It's a good idea to stay away from
linguistics. But some of those terms,

00:08:19.185 --> 00:08:20.825
you know, you end up using them.

00:08:21.095 --> 00:08:23.805
So a phoneme is just a
sound, like any sound.

00:08:23.894 --> 00:08:24.865
Uh, guh, huh, puh.

00:08:25.645 --> 00:08:27.535
Those are all phonemes from what I gather.

00:08:27.614 --> 00:08:28.175
I don't know.

00:08:28.235 --> 00:08:28.975
I use the term.

00:08:29.005 --> 00:08:30.745
I use a lot of terms that I
don't know what they mean.

00:08:31.300 --> 00:08:33.799
Uh, you know, so phonemes.

00:08:34.299 --> 00:08:38.180
I think, I think for me, it's just,
uh, I want to just enjoy the language

00:08:38.210 --> 00:08:42.530
and I like learning it and feeling
almost like prehistoric before we had

00:08:42.530 --> 00:08:45.920
all these studies. We just communicate
with people, we learn it, we don't

00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:47.540
have to see it written all the time.

00:08:47.770 --> 00:08:50.200
We don't have to know what
the conjugations mean.

00:08:50.200 --> 00:08:52.670
We just know how to say it
because we have enough exposure.

00:08:52.670 --> 00:08:54.130
And that's kind of the way I approach it.

00:08:54.130 --> 00:08:56.789
But at any rate, Arabic and
Russian are very different.

00:08:56.820 --> 00:08:59.490
Not to mention French and Spanish,
which are similar to each other.

00:08:59.850 --> 00:09:02.619
And you do very well in
all of those languages.

00:09:02.619 --> 00:09:03.389
I'm very impressed.

00:09:03.470 --> 00:09:05.090
Oh, I really appreciate that.

00:09:05.090 --> 00:09:09.130
I mean, if I could hear this back
in 2019, when I was watching your videos

00:09:09.130 --> 00:09:13.310
and almost crashing my car because I was
driving to internship interviews.

00:09:13.310 --> 00:09:16.300
And I had your YouTube
videos on while I was driving.

00:09:16.685 --> 00:09:18.985
And then I would have to change
them, you know, because they're only

00:09:18.985 --> 00:09:22.975
10, 15 minutes long, which I do not
advocate for in any sense of the word.

00:09:23.035 --> 00:09:25.955
Well, you need voice commands, voice
commands in your car, you know.

00:09:26.305 --> 00:09:29.105
Yeah, that probably would have been
better. But if I could see right now

00:09:29.105 --> 00:09:34.225
that in 2023, Steve Kaufman himself,
the LingoSteve, The Linguist would

00:09:34.234 --> 00:09:36.005
be telling me that, I don't even know.

00:09:36.295 --> 00:09:37.475
I don't know what would have happened.

00:09:37.824 --> 00:09:38.655
So I really appreciate it.

00:09:38.704 --> 00:09:43.005
And I'm just a simple guy that
started making videos, but like

00:09:43.005 --> 00:09:47.335
you and like many others, Luca and
others who are enthusiastic about

00:09:47.335 --> 00:09:50.805
language learning, we do have an
ability to influence other people.

00:09:50.815 --> 00:09:54.384
So we do good things, like it's
a good thing to learn languages.

00:09:54.385 --> 00:09:55.265
It's good for people.

00:09:55.265 --> 00:09:58.545
It's good for people's ability
to communicate with others.

00:09:58.575 --> 00:10:01.205
So it's nice to do something
that's a good thing.

00:10:01.205 --> 00:10:02.225
So you do a good thing.

00:10:02.225 --> 00:10:04.665
I think I do a good thing
in making these videos.

00:10:04.714 --> 00:10:05.304
Oh, for sure.

00:10:05.305 --> 00:10:05.585
Yeah.

00:10:05.585 --> 00:10:06.185
And I think.

00:10:06.580 --> 00:10:09.290
keeping it fun and showing
people how it can be funny.

00:10:09.450 --> 00:10:11.520
I mean, I think language
learning is inherently funny.

00:10:11.530 --> 00:10:15.220
If I walk up to a French guy and I say,
"un baguette" instead of "une baguette,"

00:10:15.400 --> 00:10:18.290
they might laugh because, you know,
our brains, psychologically, what we

00:10:18.290 --> 00:10:20.280
find funny is something unexpected.

00:10:20.594 --> 00:10:24.145
So, when someone says, "un baguette," they
might laugh. But they're not laughing

00:10:24.175 --> 00:10:25.785
at you, they're laughing with you.

00:10:25.795 --> 00:10:27.844
Yeah, but rarely do they laugh.

00:10:27.915 --> 00:10:30.155
Like, rarely do they... it's a bit jarring.

00:10:30.275 --> 00:10:34.415
It's funny that there are things, you
know, when foreigners speak a language,

00:10:34.454 --> 00:10:36.584
there are things that are not jarring.

00:10:36.625 --> 00:10:41.514
Like, an accent is not jarring, but wrong
gender, unfortunately, is a bit jarring.

00:10:41.900 --> 00:10:46.650
And, uh, I can't remember the name of
that American actress who, um, she spoke

00:10:46.660 --> 00:10:48.450
beautiful French, like pronunciation?

00:10:48.500 --> 00:10:48.910
Excellent.

00:10:49.120 --> 00:10:52.980
And actors and actresses are very
good at imitating accents, but

00:10:52.980 --> 00:10:54.750
she got most of her genders wrong.

00:10:55.170 --> 00:10:57.949
You would think 50%, you
know, she'd be correct.

00:10:58.510 --> 00:11:01.620
So genders is tough, especially in
French, because in Spanish, you got

00:11:01.620 --> 00:11:04.650
the O - A that's going to help you
along. But in French it's difficult.

00:11:05.380 --> 00:11:06.050
Oh yeah, for sure.

00:11:06.050 --> 00:11:07.040
Until you get to "un skillet," right?

00:11:07.775 --> 00:11:08.854
That's the confusing one.

00:11:08.984 --> 00:11:09.915
That one always got me.

00:11:09.984 --> 00:11:12.564
But yeah, I think that people
just need to see that it's really

00:11:12.564 --> 00:11:14.055
fun and it's not intimidating.

00:11:14.055 --> 00:11:15.364
They need to give it a go.

00:11:15.415 --> 00:11:18.244
I mean, someone who's watching this, if
you're hesitating, you're worried about

00:11:18.244 --> 00:11:21.665
judgment or anything, as Steve said,
people aren't going to laugh at you.

00:11:21.665 --> 00:11:24.305
And when they do, from my
perspective, you can laugh with them.

00:11:24.305 --> 00:11:26.294
And if it's a funny experience...

00:11:26.294 --> 00:11:26.969
And I think that...

00:11:27.119 --> 00:11:30.660
There's just so many routes to take
comedy with language learning as well

00:11:30.660 --> 00:11:34.540
because switching languages so that
someone doesn't understand you talking

00:11:34.540 --> 00:11:36.300
behind their back, all that type of stuff.

00:11:36.300 --> 00:11:37.879
It's just common tropes
that are really hilarious.

00:11:37.889 --> 00:11:40.749
I've had a few of those with Chinese,
but not as many as people think.

00:11:40.920 --> 00:11:44.279
People think if you're riding an
elevator with a group of, you know,

00:11:44.410 --> 00:11:47.129
Japanese, and they're laughing,
they're not laughing at you.

00:11:47.370 --> 00:11:50.000
You know, they have other things
to talk about besides you, but

00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:51.610
people get quite self-conscious.

00:11:51.610 --> 00:11:53.460
They think other people
are talking about them.

00:11:53.490 --> 00:11:53.900
They're not.

00:11:54.020 --> 00:11:56.550
But, uh, yeah, have fun, absolutely.

00:11:56.550 --> 00:11:59.430
And if you want to be totally
comfortable, speak your own language.

00:11:59.450 --> 00:12:01.870
When you're learning another language,
there are going to be moments that

00:12:01.870 --> 00:12:05.559
are not comfortable where you can't
remember a word and you make mistakes.

00:12:05.560 --> 00:12:07.460
And that just is part of the process.

00:12:07.590 --> 00:12:10.230
So how did you get out of
this Language Simp idea?

00:12:10.240 --> 00:12:13.900
Like I've... there are different people like
serious, like Steve Kaufman is out there

00:12:13.910 --> 00:12:15.369
seriously talking about language learning.

00:12:15.610 --> 00:12:17.100
And you do everything that's goofy.

00:12:17.130 --> 00:12:19.160
I always wanted to be a YouTuber.

00:12:19.210 --> 00:12:23.790
I've been making YouTube videos since
about 2010 or something like that.

00:12:23.850 --> 00:12:26.610
Ever since I was just, I mean,
I'm only 25, so I was, you know,

00:12:26.610 --> 00:12:29.760
just a young kid, but I always
wanted to be a YouTuber, and then I

00:12:29.760 --> 00:12:31.550
actually got into it through TikTok.

00:12:31.569 --> 00:12:36.340
When TikTok became popular, I started
making language sketches where, like,

00:12:36.340 --> 00:12:40.530
a police officer would chase me, and
I would speak different languages, so

00:12:40.540 --> 00:12:43.429
he couldn't understand me, but then,
coincidentally, the police officer

00:12:43.710 --> 00:12:48.010
knows those languages. And just just
these stupid goofy encounters like that,

00:12:48.319 --> 00:12:51.939
which were obviously scripted. But people
loved it, and they would sometimes rack up,

00:12:52.040 --> 00:12:53.740
you know, tens of millions of views.

00:12:53.749 --> 00:12:57.549
So that got me popular. Yeah, I mean
I have a short video like that of a

00:12:57.569 --> 00:13:02.199
police officer that has 50 million
views on YouTube. Like yeah, some

00:13:02.209 --> 00:13:05.485
crazy stuff. But... So people love
that, you know, someone who can switch

00:13:05.485 --> 00:13:07.505
languages in a dangerous situation.

00:13:07.635 --> 00:13:08.535
It makes it funny.

00:13:08.535 --> 00:13:13.855
And so I got popular on TikTok, got
to like a million followers on that.

00:13:13.895 --> 00:13:18.245
And then I got an engineering job,
kind of fell off. And then decided,

00:13:18.245 --> 00:13:20.895
no, I'm going to give YouTube
a try. And then it just worked.

00:13:20.904 --> 00:13:21.635
So here I am.

00:13:21.635 --> 00:13:22.954
And I'm so grateful.

00:13:22.954 --> 00:13:24.665
I mean, my childhood dream completed.

00:13:24.964 --> 00:13:26.594
So can I ask you a question?

00:13:26.604 --> 00:13:30.594
So there you are at 19,
essentially unilingual in English.

00:13:30.824 --> 00:13:31.125
Correct?

00:13:31.365 --> 00:13:34.084
And your family, you spoke just had English at home.

00:13:34.084 --> 00:13:34.245
Correct.

00:13:34.275 --> 00:13:37.045
So often, you know, my
situation was different.

00:13:37.045 --> 00:13:38.275
Obviously I was born in Sweden.

00:13:38.275 --> 00:13:39.285
We moved to Canada.

00:13:39.285 --> 00:13:40.755
I heard different languages and stuff.

00:13:41.094 --> 00:13:44.045
And so people say, well, you know, if
as a young child, you hear different

00:13:44.045 --> 00:13:45.224
languages, you have an advantage.

00:13:45.530 --> 00:13:46.300
Perhaps so.

00:13:46.709 --> 00:13:50.390
But the majority of polyglots
that I have met, and I've gone to

00:13:50.560 --> 00:13:54.930
polyglot conferences, grew up in a
monolingual, unilingual environment.

00:13:55.200 --> 00:13:59.049
And suddenly they were turned on, they
wanted to learn languages, and lo and

00:13:59.050 --> 00:14:00.980
behold, they can learn many languages.

00:14:01.099 --> 00:14:02.700
And so you are an example of that.

00:14:02.780 --> 00:14:04.249
If there's a will, there's a way.

00:14:04.320 --> 00:14:05.620
Oh, yeah, 100%.

00:14:05.640 --> 00:14:08.580
You definitely don't need any prior
exposure or anything like that.

00:14:08.580 --> 00:14:09.390
I mean, I'm proof of that.

00:14:09.390 --> 00:14:11.680
It's only been like
almost five years, but...

00:14:11.985 --> 00:14:14.915
And I feel like most of the languages
that I'm good at, I started, you

00:14:14.915 --> 00:14:17.525
know, in the first two years and
haven't even touched that much.

00:14:17.525 --> 00:14:19.305
So it's super possible.

00:14:19.305 --> 00:14:21.515
It's not that hard of
a thing for people to do.

00:14:21.815 --> 00:14:24.835
Yeah, and especially, and I mean, you're
a great example of being older and

00:14:24.835 --> 00:14:26.165
still being able to learn languages.

00:14:26.165 --> 00:14:27.835
So I hate when people make those excuses.

00:14:27.835 --> 00:14:28.974
If there's a will, there's a way.

00:14:29.194 --> 00:14:30.795
If you want to do it, you can absolutely.

00:14:31.145 --> 00:14:34.655
Okay, well, we should probably leave
it there and I'm now going to go on

00:14:34.655 --> 00:14:38.335
your channel. And I'm a bit scared
because I don't know, you know, I

00:14:38.335 --> 00:14:40.335
mean, here I can control what happens.

00:14:40.494 --> 00:14:43.955
Once I get on your channel, it's
no longer under my control. But

00:14:44.265 --> 00:14:45.225
we're going to give it a try.

00:14:45.364 --> 00:14:46.004
You're doomed.

00:14:46.495 --> 00:14:47.365
You're doomed, Steve.

